-- UKMLA Paediatrics batch 1
-- 50 questions: Growth/development & safeguarding, Neonatology & the newborn,
-- Paediatric cardiology & congenital heart disease, Paediatric emergencies,
-- Paediatric gastroenterology & nutrition, Paediatric infection & fever,
-- Paediatric respiratory disease

  'draft', 'foundation',
  $x$A 4-month-old infant, not yet able to roll or sit, is brought to the emergency department by his mother because she 'noticed some bruises' while changing his nappy. Examination reveals five bruises of differing colours (yellow, green, and purple) over the trunk, upper arms, and buttocks. There is no history of a fall, and the mother says she is unsure how they occurred. The infant is otherwise well, feeding normally, and developmentally appropriate for age.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate next step?',
  array['safeguarding','non-accidental injury','bruising']
),
('e1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 'ukmla', '9d05fb0b-2696-44ec-8350-15a6911bee57',
  'draft', 'core',
  $x$An 18-month-old boy is brought to his GP for a routine check. His parents report he is not yet walking independently, although he can pull to stand and cruise around furniture. He said his first words ('mama', 'dada') at 12 months but has not gained any new words since. He points to indicate what he wants and enjoys peekaboo games. He was born at term with no antenatal or perinatal concerns.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate next step?',
  array['developmental delay','milestones','referral']
),
('e1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003', 'ukmla', '9d05fb0b-2696-44ec-8350-15a6911bee57',
  'draft', 'foundation',
  $x$A 13-year-old boy is referred by his GP for short stature. His height is on the 2nd centile, but his growth velocity has been steady and parallel to the 2nd centile for the past three years. His weight is proportionate. Pubertal staging shows testicular volume of 3 mL (pre-pubertal). His father reports he 'was also a late developer' and did not have his growth spurt until age 16. Bone age is read as delayed by approximately 2 years relative to chronological age.$x$,
  'What is the most likely explanation for these findings?',
  array['short stature','constitutional delay','puberty']
),
('e1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000004', 'ukmla', '9d05fb0b-2696-44ec-8350-15a6911bee57',
  'draft', 'core',
  $x$A 7-month-old infant who is not yet independently mobile (cannot crawl) is brought to the emergency department after his mother reports he 'seemed to cry more than usual' when his leg was being dressed. There is no history of trauma. Examination shows reduced movement and mild swelling of the left thigh. An X-ray of the femur shows a metaphyseal fracture with a corner/bucket-handle appearance. There is no relevant birth history and no known bone disease.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['non-accidental injury','metaphyseal fracture','safeguarding']
),
('e1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000005', 'ukmla', '9d05fb0b-2696-44ec-8350-15a6911bee57',
  'draft', 'foundation',
  'An 18-month-old girl is seen at her routine health visitor check. Her parents report that she does not respond to her name being called, does not point to show interest in objects, avoids eye contact, and has not developed any spoken words. She lines up her toy cars repeatedly and becomes distressed when this routine is disrupted. Her hearing screen at birth was normal, and her motor milestones (walking at 13 months) were age-appropriate.',
  'Which combination of features is most suggestive of the underlying diagnosis?',
  array['autism spectrum disorder','developmental red flags']
),
('e1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000006', 'ukmla', '9d05fb0b-2696-44ec-8350-15a6911bee57',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  $x$A 6-week-old infant is brought to the emergency department by ambulance after being found unresponsive and floppy at home. He was born at term with an uncomplicated delivery. On examination he is drowsy with a bulging fontanelle and is not moving his left arm and leg as much as the right. There are no external bruises or marks. CT head shows a subdural haematoma with associated retinal haemorrhages confirmed on fundoscopy by ophthalmology. The parents deny any trauma and say he was 'just fussy' before becoming unresponsive.$x$,
  'What is the most likely underlying diagnosis?',
  array['abusive head trauma','shaken baby syndrome','safeguarding']
),
('e1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000007', 'ukmla', '9d05fb0b-2696-44ec-8350-15a6911bee57',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 9-month-old boy is brought to his GP for a routine review. His growth chart shows that his weight has fallen from the 50th centile at birth to the 2nd centile now, having crossed two major centile lines over the past six months. He is formula-fed, and his mother reports he takes only small volumes and often vomits after feeds. Developmental milestones are appropriate for age. There is no history of diarrhoea, and examination is otherwise unremarkable other than reduced subcutaneous fat.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step?',
  array['faltering growth','failure to thrive','growth chart']
),
('e1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008', 'ukmla', '024e7793-a695-4463-8763-fa1f229ec965',
  'draft', 'foundation',
  $x$A baby boy is noted to be jaundiced 12 hours after birth. He was born at term by normal vaginal delivery to a mother who is blood group O rhesus-positive; the baby's blood group is A rhesus-positive. Direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test is positive. Total serum bilirubin is significantly elevated and plotted above the treatment threshold on the bilirubin chart for his gestation and age.$x$,
  'What is the most likely underlying cause of this jaundice?',
  array['neonatal jaundice','haemolysis','ABO incompatibility']
),
('e1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000009', 'ukmla', '024e7793-a695-4463-8763-fa1f229ec965',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A term baby girl, now 16 days old, remains visibly jaundiced. She is exclusively breastfed, feeding well, and gaining weight appropriately. Her stools are noted by the mother to be pale/chalky in colour and her urine appears dark. On examination she is well, alert, and not dysmorphic. Total and split bilirubin is requested.',
  'Which additional investigation finding would be most concerning and require urgent referral?',
  array['prolonged neonatal jaundice','biliary atresia','conjugated bilirubin']
),
('e1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000010', 'ukmla', '024e7793-a695-4463-8763-fa1f229ec965',
  'draft', 'core',
  $x$A baby boy is born at 39 weeks' gestation by vaginal delivery. His mother had a positive vaginal swab for group B streptococcus in this pregnancy but did not receive intrapartum antibiotic prophylaxis because labour progressed rapidly. At 6 hours of age, the baby is noted to be lethargic, grunting, and tachypnoeic with a respiratory rate of 70/min, and his temperature is 35.9C. He is otherwise appropriately grown for gestational age.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management?',
  array['early-onset neonatal sepsis','group B streptococcus','antibiotics']
),
('e1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011', 'ukmla', '024e7793-a695-4463-8763-fa1f229ec965',
  'draft', 'foundation',
  $x$A baby girl is born at 30 weeks' gestation by emergency caesarean section for maternal pre-eclampsia. Antenatal steroids were not given as delivery was too rapid. Within minutes of birth she develops grunting, nasal flaring, subcostal recession, and tachypnoea. Oxygen saturations are 88% in air. Chest X-ray shows a diffuse 'ground-glass' appearance with air bronchograms.$x$,
  'What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  array['respiratory distress syndrome','prematurity','surfactant deficiency']
),
('e1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012', 'ukmla', '024e7793-a695-4463-8763-fa1f229ec965',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A baby boy is born at term to a mother with type 1 diabetes mellitus, whose glycaemic control was suboptimal throughout pregnancy. He is large for gestational age at birth. At 90 minutes of age he is noted to be jittery, floppy, and reluctant to feed. His capillary blood glucose is 1.6 mmol/L.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management?',
  array['neonatal hypoglycaemia','infant of diabetic mother']
),
('e1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013', 'ukmla', '024e7793-a695-4463-8763-fa1f229ec965',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  $x$A baby girl born at 27 weeks' gestation is now 12 days old on the neonatal unit, established on increasing volumes of enteral feeds. She develops abdominal distension, bilious vomiting, and blood-stained stools. She is lethargic with temperature instability and increasing episodes of apnoea. Abdominal X-ray shows dilated bowel loops with pneumatosis intestinalis (intramural gas).$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management?',
  array['necrotising enterocolitis','prematurity','surgical emergency']
),
('e1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014', 'ukmla', '024e7793-a695-4463-8763-fa1f229ec965',
  'draft', 'core',
  $x$A baby boy is born at 41 weeks' gestation after a prolonged, complicated labour with thick meconium-stained liquor and a period of fetal bradycardia. At birth he is floppy, apnoeic, and requires prolonged resuscitation with a 10-minute Apgar score of 4. Cord gas shows a severe metabolic acidosis. Over the next hours he develops abnormal tone, reduced consciousness, and subtle seizure activity.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management once he is stabilised?',
  array['hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy','therapeutic hypothermia']
),
('e1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015', 'ukmla', 'c52da48c-ab4d-40ed-85e1-176e75336293',
  'draft', 'foundation',
  'A 6-week-old infant is brought for a routine check. She is thriving, feeding well, and has normal growth. On examination there is a harsh pansystolic murmur loudest at the left lower sternal border, with no radiation to the back, and no cyanosis. She is otherwise asymptomatic. An echocardiogram confirms a small (3 mm) ventricular septal defect with no evidence of pulmonary hypertension or significant left-to-right shunt.',
  'What is the most appropriate management?',
  array['ventricular septal defect','congenital heart disease']
),
('e1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016', 'ukmla', 'c52da48c-ab4d-40ed-85e1-176e75336293',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 4-month-old infant with known tetralogy of Fallot, awaiting surgical repair, becomes acutely more blue and breathless while crying during a vaccination visit. He is inconsolable, cyanosed, and tachypnoeic, and the intensity of his previously noted ejection systolic murmur has decreased.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management?',
  array['tetralogy of Fallot','tet spell','cyanotic congenital heart disease']
),
