  'draft', 'core',
  'A baby boy, born at term and discharged home well after a normal newborn examination, is brought to the emergency department at 4 days of age with poor feeding, grey-blue colour, and lethargy. On examination he is tachypnoeic, has weak pulses throughout, and cool peripheries. Pre- and post-ductal oxygen saturations are both low with a significant gradient between them. A hyperoxia test shows minimal improvement in saturations with 100% oxygen.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management while awaiting definitive cardiology assessment?',
  array['duct-dependent congenital heart disease','prostaglandin E1','neonatal collapse']
),
('e1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000020', 'ukmla', 'c52da48c-ab4d-40ed-85e1-176e75336293',
  'draft', 'foundation',
  'A 5-year-old boy is referred by his school nurse after a soft murmur was noted during a routine check. He is asymptomatic, thriving, and fully active in sports. On examination there is a soft, short systolic murmur heard best at the left sternal edge, which varies with position (softer when standing, louder when supine) and has no radiation, no diastolic component, and is not associated with a thrill. Peripheral pulses, heart sounds, and the rest of the cardiovascular examination are entirely normal.',
  'What is the most appropriate management?',
  array['innocent murmur','paediatric examination']
),
('e1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000021', 'ukmla', 'c52da48c-ab4d-40ed-85e1-176e75336293',
  'draft', 'core',
  $x$A 3-year-old girl has had a fever of 39.5C for 6 days, unresponsive to antibiotics given by her GP for a presumed throat infection. On examination she has bilateral non-purulent conjunctival injection, a diffuse erythematous rash, cracked erythematous lips, a 'strawberry' tongue, and swelling and erythema of both hands and feet. There is a single palpable, non-tender cervical lymph node measuring 2 cm.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management?',
  array['Kawasaki disease','coronary artery aneurysm','IVIG']
),
('e1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000022', 'ukmla', 'ca9968cf-6845-4d5c-bc8c-c1ac6791b4a0',
  'draft', 'foundation',
  'A previously well 20-month-old girl is brought to the emergency department after a witnessed generalised tonic-clonic seizure lasting 3 minutes at home, occurring during a fever of 38.9C associated with an upper respiratory tract infection. She has fully recovered, is alert and playing, and there are no focal neurological signs. This is her first such episode, and there is no history of recent travel, rash, or reduced consciousness prior to the seizure.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step?',
  array['febrile seizure','simple febrile convulsion']
),
('e1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000023', 'ukmla', 'ca9968cf-6845-4d5c-bc8c-c1ac6791b4a0',
  'draft', 'foundation',
  'A 3-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department by ambulance with a high fever, lethargy, and a non-blanching purpuric rash spreading over his legs and trunk. He is tachycardic, has cool peripheries, and a capillary refill time of 4 seconds. He is difficult to rouse. The paramedics have already established intravenous access.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management?',
  array['meningococcal sepsis','non-blanching rash','septic shock']
),
('e1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000024', 'ukmla', 'ca9968cf-6845-4d5c-bc8c-c1ac6791b4a0',
  'draft', 'foundation',
  'An 8-year-old boy with a known peanut allergy accidentally eats a biscuit containing peanut traces at school. Within minutes he develops widespread urticaria, facial and lip swelling, audible wheeze, and difficulty breathing. He is anxious, and his voice sounds hoarse. His blood pressure is 78/40 mmHg.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management?',
  array['anaphylaxis','adrenaline','allergic emergency']
